1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,156
Last lecture ended on a question: which lever actually earns its engineering cost

2
00:00:05,156 --> 00:00:06,639
for a feature like yours.

3
00:00:06,639 --> 00:00:11,623
Here are the three real candidates, and the honest answer for each is "it depends on

4
00:00:11,623 --> 00:00:16,548
your traffic," not "always" or "never." None of these change what the model can do.

5
00:00:16,548 --> 00:00:21,413
They change what you pay for the same output, and each one only pays off against a

6
00:00:21,413 --> 00:00:24,735
specific pattern in how your feature actually gets used.

7
00:00:24,735 --> 00:00:28,651
That distinction is worth holding onto through this whole lecture.

8
00:00:28,651 --> 00:00:32,686
Every one of these three is invisible to the end user when it works.

9
00:00:32,686 --> 00:00:37,610
Nobody notices a cache hit, a batched job that finished overnight, or a router that

10
00:00:37,610 --> 00:00:40,517
quietly sent an easy question to a cheaper model.

11
00:00:40,517 --> 00:00:44,077
The entire value shows up on an invoice, not in the product.

12
00:00:44,627 --> 00:00:49,583
Read the right column as a checklist, not a sales pitch, because building any of

13
00:00:49,583 --> 00:00:54,787
these for traffic that doesn't match the pattern is real engineering time spent on a

14
00:00:54,787 --> 00:00:56,335
discount nobody collects.

15
00:00:56,335 --> 00:00:59,805
Caching is the direct fix for something you already met.

16
00:00:59,805 --> 00:01:05,008
Remember 7.1's resent-history table: the same system prompt and growing conversation

17
00:01:05,008 --> 00:01:08,539
history gets billed again, in full, on every single turn.

18
00:01:08,539 --> 00:01:13,743
Most providers now discount tokens that repeat exactly from the previous call, often

19
00:01:13,743 --> 00:01:14,238
steeply.

20
00:01:14,238 --> 00:01:19,318
That discount applies specifically to the resent-history problem, not to genuinely

21
00:01:19,318 --> 00:01:24,336
new content, which is why caching helps a long conversation far more than a short

22
00:01:24,336 --> 00:01:24,584
one.

23
00:01:24,584 --> 00:01:28,920
Apply it to last lecture's own numbers and the shape becomes concrete.

24
00:01:28,920 --> 00:01:33,752
Most of that eight thousand input tokens per conversation is system prompt and

25
00:01:33,752 --> 00:01:37,407
resent history, the exact content a cache discount targets.

26
00:01:37,407 --> 00:01:41,124
Only the newest turn's message is genuinely fresh each time.

27
00:01:41,124 --> 00:01:46,204
Cache the repeated portion and the input side of that conversation's cost drops by

28
00:01:46,204 --> 00:01:51,407
roughly a third, without touching a single word of what the feature actually says to

29
00:01:51,407 --> 00:01:51,965
the user.

30
00:01:52,515 --> 00:01:57,773
Same technique, same discount, and the verdict flips entirely based on one question:

31
00:01:57,773 --> 00:02:01,153
is anyone actually waiting on this response right now.

32
00:02:01,153 --> 00:02:05,973
That single question sorts most of a feature's traffic faster than any deeper

33
00:02:05,973 --> 00:02:06,536
analysis.

34
00:02:06,536 --> 00:02:11,418
A support chat, a live drafting tool, anything with a person watching a cursor

35
00:02:11,418 --> 00:02:16,489
blink, rules out batching immediately, whatever the discount looks like on paper.

36
00:02:16,489 --> 00:02:21,559
A weekly digest, a bulk re-summarisation job, anything nobody's refreshing a page

37
00:02:21,559 --> 00:02:24,313
for, is exactly what batching was built for.

38
00:02:24,313 --> 00:02:29,196
Model routing has its own version of this question: does your query difficulty

39
00:02:29,196 --> 00:02:30,072
actually vary?

40
00:02:30,072 --> 00:02:35,330
It only pays off if it does, and if you can detect that cheaply before committing to

41
00:02:35,330 --> 00:02:36,582
the expensive model.

42
00:02:36,582 --> 00:02:41,840
A feature where every query needs your strongest model's reasoning gets nothing from

43
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:46,973
routing except the cost of maintaining a router that always picks the same answer.

44
00:02:46,973 --> 00:02:51,855
The trap with routing specifically is that the classifier deciding "easy" from

45
00:02:51,855 --> 00:02:57,051
"hard" is itself a model call, and a badly built one can misroute expensive queries

46
00:02:57,051 --> 00:03:01,557
to the cheap model and quietly tank quality right where it matters most.

47
00:03:01,557 --> 00:03:06,440
Section four's whole discipline applies here directly: you need an eval on the

48
00:03:06,440 --> 00:03:11,510
routing decision itself, not just on the two models it's choosing between, or you

49
00:03:11,510 --> 00:03:15,078
won't know it's wrong until a user notices before you do.

50
00:03:15,628 --> 00:03:20,630
Say the honest caveat, because it's tempting to greenlight all three at once, since

51
00:03:20,630 --> 00:03:22,921
each one has a clean story on its own.

52
00:03:22,921 --> 00:03:27,863
A cache that's rarely hit, a batch queue processing requests nobody was willing to

53
00:03:27,863 --> 00:03:32,865
wait for, and a router constantly sending traffic to the expensive model anyway are

54
00:03:32,865 --> 00:03:36,783
three real maintenance burdens for close to zero combined saving.

55
00:03:36,783 --> 00:03:41,725
Pick the lever that matches a pattern you can actually point to in your own usage,

56
00:03:41,725 --> 00:03:46,727
build that one properly, and leave the other two for the traffic pattern that would

57
00:03:46,727 --> 00:03:47,511
justify them.

58
00:03:47,511 --> 00:03:52,453
This is the same instinct as 6.4's guardrails lecture, aimed at engineering effort

59
00:03:52,453 --> 00:03:53,417
instead of risk.

60
00:03:53,417 --> 00:03:58,419
Spend the budget where the pattern is real, not evenly across every option that has

61
00:03:58,419 --> 00:04:00,047
a plausible-sounding story.

62
00:04:00,047 --> 00:04:04,868
A team that ships one well-matched lever this quarter beats one that ships three

63
00:04:04,868 --> 00:04:07,520
half-built ones nobody finishes maintaining.

64
00:04:08,070 --> 00:04:13,070
That's the test worth keeping, and it's deliberately more boring than the individual

65
00:04:13,070 --> 00:04:17,118
techniques, because boring and checkable beats exciting and assumed.

66
00:04:17,118 --> 00:04:19,797
Run it against real numbers if you have them.

67
00:04:19,797 --> 00:04:23,012
How often does the exact same context actually repeat.

68
00:04:23,012 --> 00:04:26,583
How much of your traffic could genuinely wait until tonight.

69
00:04:26,583 --> 00:04:30,631
How much does query difficulty actually vary, measured, not guessed.

70
00:04:30,631 --> 00:04:34,381
Each answer points at exactly one lever, usually not all three.

71
00:04:34,381 --> 00:04:39,144
If you don't have real numbers yet, say so, the same discipline as every guessed

72
00:04:39,144 --> 00:04:40,513
figure in this section.

73
00:04:40,513 --> 00:04:45,215
"I expect caching to help based on how the feature is designed" is a legitimate

74
00:04:45,215 --> 00:04:46,108
starting point.

75
00:04:46,108 --> 00:04:50,930
Presenting that expectation as a measured saving before anyone's checked is where

76
00:04:50,930 --> 00:04:55,573
this exercise quietly turns into wishful thinking with a spreadsheet attached.

77
00:04:56,123 --> 00:04:57,917
One thing before you move on.

78
00:04:57,917 --> 00:05:03,052
Check the repetition question first, because caching is the lever most features can

79
00:05:03,052 --> 00:05:08,001
adopt with the least new engineering, and it's the direct answer to the specific

80
00:05:08,001 --> 00:05:09,300
waste 7.1 identified.

81
00:05:09,300 --> 00:05:12,083
Then name one lever, not a wishlist of three.

82
00:05:12,083 --> 00:05:17,280
Say it plainly if the honest answer right now is none of them, because that's a real

83
00:05:17,280 --> 00:05:22,229
finding too, and a much better one than building a lever nobody's usage actually

84
00:05:22,229 --> 00:05:22,847
justifies.

85
00:05:22,847 --> 00:05:27,796
Next lecture goes deeper on the harder version of the routing question: not just

86
00:05:27,796 --> 00:05:32,869
cheap versus expensive, but which specific model actually earns its price for your

87
00:05:32,869 --> 00:05:37,013
feature, scored properly instead of guessed from a benchmark table.
